1
00:00:00,040 --> 00:00:02,120
And now, for you, Scoutmaster.

2
00:00:08,260 --> 00:00:13,880
So I was thinking about this, and I wonder if when mosquitoes go camping, they complain about all the people.

3
00:00:15,440 --> 00:00:17,140
I don't know. What do you think?

4
00:00:17,820 --> 00:00:19,980
Hey, this is podcast number 53.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:54,540
Welcome back to the Scoutmaster Podcast. This is Clark Green.

6
00:00:54,540 --> 00:00:59,680
I wanted to let you know that I have an interview scheduled tomorrow morning with Cliff Jacobson.

7
00:01:00,180 --> 00:01:09,660
And Cliff is a very respected outdoors author, a guide, and I am really looking forward to speaking with him.

8
00:01:09,860 --> 00:01:14,000
And hopefully we'll have that interview on next week's podcast.

9
00:01:15,400 --> 00:01:18,320
So a lot of you got in touch this week. Let me see here.

10
00:01:18,320 --> 00:01:27,420
Arlen, thanks for the retweet on podcast number 52 and the Scoutmaster newsletter, which went out last week.

11
00:01:27,480 --> 00:01:29,620
The January edition went out last week.

12
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:42,740
And there's always a giveaway in the Scoutmaster newsletter, and I will let you know who won that next week because the giveaway is open for entries until tomorrow, I think.

13
00:01:43,640 --> 00:01:46,100
Tim got in touch about the newsletter.

14
00:01:46,100 --> 00:01:51,919
And the content of the newsletter, the focus of the newsletter this time was special needs scouting.

15
00:01:52,539 --> 00:01:56,399
And Tim had this to say. He says, thanks for starting up your newsletter.

16
00:01:56,860 --> 00:02:00,140
Your writings on special needs scouting were very timely for me today.

17
00:02:00,539 --> 00:02:03,520
This weekend on a campout, I had a Scout approach me for a hug.

18
00:02:04,220 --> 00:02:08,079
It was the first time that had ever happened, and it was a bit awkward, to tell you the truth.

19
00:02:08,080 --> 00:02:14,540
My mind was racing with youth protection concerns as to how this might appear to others in the room.

20
00:02:15,340 --> 00:02:22,080
And what you said about hugs are for families and scouts shake hands sounds like a great approach.

21
00:02:22,380 --> 00:02:25,080
And if I ever run into this again, I'll give it a try.

22
00:02:25,540 --> 00:02:29,000
Tim, I'm glad that you found that information useful. I really am.

23
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:33,160
James, a Scoutmaster from Haymarket, Virginia, sent me this story.

24
00:02:33,940 --> 00:02:39,020
He said, three years ago, a Scout with Down syndrome bridged over from Weebelos into our troop.

25
00:02:39,660 --> 00:02:44,840
His parents were a little skeptical about scouting as other troops were kind of waving them off.

26
00:02:45,300 --> 00:02:49,600
And I told them that the other troops were wrong, and I welcomed this great opportunity.

27
00:02:50,740 --> 00:02:54,980
This Scout was functioning at about a third grade level, and he had some physical challenges.

28
00:02:55,400 --> 00:02:57,640
His first summer camp was a great success.

29
00:02:57,640 --> 00:03:03,239
You know, as a matter of fact, he fared better than my son, and even earned more merit badges than he did.

30
00:03:04,079 --> 00:03:12,239
Our Scouts may have gotten more out of working with this Scout, whose name was Rusty, than Rusty ever got out of scouting.

31
00:03:13,059 --> 00:03:23,179
As years passed, I kept in touch with his parents, and eventually we came to the conclusion that Rusty just was not keeping up with us and within our troop.

32
00:03:23,180 --> 00:03:27,920
So we started looking around to try to find a troop that could better suit his needs.

33
00:03:28,680 --> 00:03:31,620
I found an active troop that worked with boys like Rusty.

34
00:03:32,180 --> 00:03:38,960
And Rusty and his parents and I visited them and decided that this was a great place for Rusty to continue in scouting.

35
00:03:39,460 --> 00:03:43,600
They have several members with Down syndrome, including the Scoutmaster's son.

36
00:03:43,600 --> 00:03:50,780
This past summer, as a part of my diversity ticket for wood badge, my troop and Rusty's new troop planned a joint activity.

37
00:03:51,480 --> 00:03:56,040
Our youth leadership met with theirs, and they planned the event together.

38
00:03:56,500 --> 00:04:02,519
We held a joint troop meeting and a Saturday session to work on the historic carpentry merit badge.

39
00:04:02,520 --> 00:04:08,160
Scouts formed a group with members from both troops, and about 30 scouts completed the merit badge that day.

40
00:04:08,240 --> 00:04:09,460
What a production that was.

41
00:04:10,100 --> 00:04:14,300
We formed some friendships and planned more joint activities with this troop.

42
00:04:14,540 --> 00:04:16,680
James, thanks so much for sending me the story.

43
00:04:17,200 --> 00:04:18,960
That's very inspirational.

44
00:04:19,300 --> 00:04:23,300
And it also points something out that I think is important to realize.

45
00:04:23,300 --> 00:04:32,640
There is a point in mainstreaming boys with some special needs where you might come to a juncture and say,

46
00:04:32,860 --> 00:04:41,700
well, you know, what we're doing here is not answering the needs that this particular Scout needs.

47
00:04:41,920 --> 00:04:47,180
Jim, I've got to give you a lot of credit for understanding that and for assessing the situation

48
00:04:47,180 --> 00:04:50,380
and then making sure that Rusty could continue on in scouting.

49
00:04:50,380 --> 00:04:53,360
That's a great story, and I really do appreciate it.

50
00:04:53,500 --> 00:04:55,540
I also had this message from Doug who says this,

51
00:04:55,640 --> 00:04:59,520
We have three scouts in our troop, and these three have been members of patrol since September.

52
00:05:00,600 --> 00:05:05,080
And one is a tenderfoot, and two are scouts.

53
00:05:05,740 --> 00:05:08,580
In February, we have five scouts crossing over from Cub Scouts,

54
00:05:08,659 --> 00:05:14,159
and I'm looking for opinions or guidance on whether we should have the five join the existing patrol

55
00:05:14,159 --> 00:05:19,580
and reach the magic number eight, or if we should just have a new patrol, one having three and one having five.

56
00:05:19,580 --> 00:05:22,320
Well, Doug, since you asked, here's my advice.

57
00:05:22,859 --> 00:05:26,260
I think I've boiled things down to two basic rules of thumb on patrols.

58
00:05:26,760 --> 00:05:32,840
First is that the most important thing for scouts is they want to be in a patrol with their buddies,

59
00:05:33,060 --> 00:05:34,780
and I think that's our first consideration.

60
00:05:34,780 --> 00:05:42,840
So that would be one of the factors that I would want to discover, see who was buddies with who,

61
00:05:43,239 --> 00:05:45,059
and make sure they all got to be in the same patrol.

62
00:05:45,159 --> 00:05:50,059
That being said, patrols do function best with at least six but no more than ten members.

63
00:05:50,239 --> 00:05:52,460
Eight is, as you say, the magic number.

64
00:05:52,460 --> 00:05:58,180
So my bias would be towards making sure that I have a patrol of at least eight,

65
00:05:58,760 --> 00:06:03,460
because patrols of three and five, historically for me, they don't work very well,

66
00:06:03,659 --> 00:06:05,560
because there's always one guy missing.

67
00:06:06,240 --> 00:06:09,359
And if it's a patrol of three, well, now you're down to one guy.

68
00:06:09,880 --> 00:06:11,799
If it's a patrol of five, you're down to four.

69
00:06:12,179 --> 00:06:14,280
And a lot of times, you know, the more guys in a patrol,

70
00:06:14,400 --> 00:06:17,740
the more chance you have of a guy missing a particular meeting or event.

71
00:06:17,740 --> 00:06:22,360
So fighting strength seems to be about eight guys, no more than ten.

72
00:06:22,840 --> 00:06:24,980
You know, rule of thumb, no fewer than six.

73
00:06:25,380 --> 00:06:28,960
None of this is official policy or rules or anything like that.

74
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:30,780
It's just rules of thumb, in my opinion.

75
00:06:31,100 --> 00:06:32,780
And that's what you asked for, wasn't it, Doug?

76
00:06:33,220 --> 00:06:34,840
So thanks for being in touch.

77
00:06:35,300 --> 00:06:38,900
Today we're going to be talking a little bit about venture patrols.

78
00:06:39,600 --> 00:06:40,520
Venture patrols.

79
00:06:40,560 --> 00:06:41,440
Not venture crews.

80
00:06:41,580 --> 00:06:42,360
Venture patrols.

81
00:06:42,360 --> 00:06:47,080
This is an outgrowth of a discussion that we've been having over the last couple of podcasts,

82
00:06:47,080 --> 00:06:50,060
but we'll talk about that in Scoutmaster Ship in seven minutes or less.

83
00:06:50,240 --> 00:06:51,659
Then we got an email to answer.

84
00:06:52,419 --> 00:06:53,880
That sounds like a lot to get done.

85
00:06:54,140 --> 00:06:55,400
So let's get started, shall we?

86
00:06:55,520 --> 00:06:59,840
Scoutmaster Ship in seven minutes or less.

87
00:07:06,700 --> 00:07:10,659
Over the past couple of podcasts, we've been talking about patrols and how patrols are made up.

88
00:07:10,660 --> 00:07:20,160
I mentioned in our last podcast, number 52, that we had been using this kind of loose venture patrol model for a long time.

89
00:07:20,540 --> 00:07:21,780
And I got a couple of comments.

90
00:07:21,900 --> 00:07:23,040
One from Larry Geiger.

91
00:07:23,180 --> 00:07:23,800
Thanks, Larry.

92
00:07:24,020 --> 00:07:25,300
Thanks for being in touch again.

93
00:07:26,120 --> 00:07:31,080
He says, just to comment on podcast 52 concerning venture activities.

94
00:07:31,640 --> 00:07:33,960
I think what you're describing is a venture patrol.

95
00:07:34,580 --> 00:07:36,960
A venture crew is a chartered unit of scouting.

96
00:07:36,960 --> 00:07:41,280
And I did call it a venture crew in the last podcast.

97
00:07:41,560 --> 00:07:44,280
So this is trying to clear up a little bit of confusion.

98
00:07:44,719 --> 00:07:47,320
It doesn't really matter to me what you call it, Larry says.

99
00:07:47,520 --> 00:07:53,380
But to have a venture crew, you do, you know, have to have a committee, a venture crew leader, a charter, and everything like that.

100
00:07:53,740 --> 00:07:57,599
So we're not trying to get stuck in terminology here, just like Larry says.

101
00:07:57,700 --> 00:08:00,620
But this is a pretty important little piece here.

102
00:08:00,620 --> 00:08:07,880
And what Larry sent me were a couple of links to some descriptions of venture patrols.

103
00:08:07,940 --> 00:08:13,540
And I'm going to have those links on the blog post that contains this podcast.

104
00:08:14,040 --> 00:08:15,900
So you'll want to go and check those out.

105
00:08:16,120 --> 00:08:20,800
Larry goes on to say, we've had the venture patrol in both of my troops, but I've never used the venture patch.

106
00:08:20,800 --> 00:08:30,800
Now you'll see if you follow the links that I provide you, there is a venture patch available for the uniform that indicates that a scout is part of the venture patrol.

107
00:08:31,120 --> 00:08:40,539
The venture patrol usually becomes active in my troops in September or October when we start planning our Appalachian Trail 50 miler every two years.

108
00:08:41,039 --> 00:08:42,980
They meet throughout the year to get ready for the hike.

109
00:08:43,380 --> 00:08:45,300
They elect a patrol leader and a quartermaster.

110
00:08:45,300 --> 00:08:50,300
All the scouts and adults going get together about once a month outside of regular scout meetings.

111
00:08:50,819 --> 00:08:53,740
All the money collected for the trip goes through the regular troop treasury.

112
00:08:54,520 --> 00:08:56,300
And then Walter also commented.

113
00:08:57,060 --> 00:08:59,579
He said, we do exactly what you do for the venture patrol.

114
00:09:00,180 --> 00:09:03,819
It is a virtual patrol that exists purely for high adventure outings.

115
00:09:04,319 --> 00:09:06,479
They're still members of their regular patrols.

116
00:09:06,560 --> 00:09:10,240
The right terminology is venture patrol and not crew.

117
00:09:10,319 --> 00:09:14,219
You know, I'm really thankful that there are guys out there who are smarter than me.

118
00:09:15,300 --> 00:09:17,800
Thank you, Walter and Larry, for putting me straight on that.

119
00:09:18,300 --> 00:09:19,579
A crew is a chartered unit.

120
00:09:20,260 --> 00:09:23,120
The leader is venture patrol leader.

121
00:09:23,680 --> 00:09:28,540
That is a leadership position that qualifies for Eagle, so it's worth using the right terminology.

122
00:09:28,939 --> 00:09:30,579
You are exactly right, my friend.

123
00:09:31,260 --> 00:09:36,979
Members can wear the venture patch, certainly one of the more obscure bits of the official uniform.

124
00:09:36,980 --> 00:09:41,620
The Scoutmaster Handbook has a whopping one or two paragraphs about venture patrols,

125
00:09:41,680 --> 00:09:46,980
but the scheme is essentially a return to explorers in the troop from the 1950s.

126
00:09:47,160 --> 00:09:52,880
This 60-year-old diagram is much more helpful than what is in the current handbook,

127
00:09:53,460 --> 00:09:58,660
and Walter provided me a link to an ancient piece of scouting literature

128
00:09:58,660 --> 00:10:04,560
that describes the relationship between this older boy patrol within a troop.

129
00:10:04,560 --> 00:10:11,239
And I think it is, as Walter says, a pretty easy way of looking at and picturing this,

130
00:10:11,339 --> 00:10:13,439
and I'll have that link on the blog.

131
00:10:13,959 --> 00:10:15,300
What exactly are we talking about?

132
00:10:15,420 --> 00:10:19,219
Well, you can establish a venture patrol within your troop.

133
00:10:19,339 --> 00:10:23,800
This qualifies the scouts who are members of that patrol to wear the venture strip,

134
00:10:23,959 --> 00:10:28,839
which is a badge that goes on their uniform, and it just says venture on it.

135
00:10:28,840 --> 00:10:33,900
The other interesting thing is, is that there is actually a venture patrol letter.

136
00:10:34,760 --> 00:10:35,480
That's right.

137
00:10:35,700 --> 00:10:40,580
It looks like a letter like you would get in high school for being a part of an athletic team.

138
00:10:40,900 --> 00:10:41,640
It's a big V.

139
00:10:42,200 --> 00:10:48,140
And there are pins associated with activities that you can get to put on that letter.

140
00:10:48,620 --> 00:10:52,440
Now, I'm not personally using this, but I wanted to point it out,

141
00:10:52,560 --> 00:10:56,160
just to know that that program exists, and it's something that you can work with.

142
00:10:56,160 --> 00:11:01,600
Some of you will see that this is very similar to the varsity scout letter.

143
00:11:02,020 --> 00:11:07,400
Varsity scouting is, you know, kind of like a not-so-well-known program within scouting,

144
00:11:07,500 --> 00:11:09,339
and it's not used in a whole lot of places.

145
00:11:09,699 --> 00:11:13,439
The reason that the venture patrol letter looks similar to the varsity letter

146
00:11:13,439 --> 00:11:15,780
is because it is exactly the same thing.

147
00:11:16,380 --> 00:11:22,000
So, just a little program note there to know that, hey, there's some badges and pins that can be worked with,

148
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:26,080
and follow the links on the blog, and you'll be able to see what I'm talking about.

149
00:11:26,080 --> 00:11:33,340
Now, I will reiterate that Walter and Larry and myself use this venture patrol

150
00:11:33,340 --> 00:11:37,500
to organize and arrange high adventure trips.

151
00:11:38,160 --> 00:11:44,920
Larry does a once-every-two-years 50-miler Appalachian trail hike using the venture patrol concept.

152
00:11:44,920 --> 00:11:47,180
We do an annual outing.

153
00:11:47,740 --> 00:11:51,300
Usually, it's up to Canada for a canoe trip,

154
00:11:52,020 --> 00:11:55,500
or this year we're going over to Kondersteg in Switzerland,

155
00:11:55,880 --> 00:11:58,839
and we're using the venture patrol model around this.

156
00:11:59,120 --> 00:12:02,420
Now, I have incorrectly called it a venture crew,

157
00:12:03,260 --> 00:12:05,880
but it is actually a venture patrol.

158
00:12:06,219 --> 00:12:11,900
So, you encounter every once in a while little bits of scouting that are not really well-defined,

159
00:12:11,900 --> 00:12:16,420
and I hope this helps lend a little bit of definition to it.

160
00:12:16,880 --> 00:12:20,480
The boys in our troop, who are members of the venture patrol,

161
00:12:21,060 --> 00:12:25,280
that venture patrol does not exist within the troop as an official,

162
00:12:25,980 --> 00:12:30,020
like, ongoing patrol that meets every time patrols meet.

163
00:12:30,220 --> 00:12:36,100
This is an identification for scouts who are participating in this high adventure activity.

164
00:12:36,260 --> 00:12:39,660
They usually have to be a minimum age of 13 to do this,

165
00:12:39,660 --> 00:12:45,079
and they get together outside of the regular meetings of the troop

166
00:12:45,079 --> 00:12:47,020
to plan and arrange their activities.

167
00:12:47,360 --> 00:12:51,520
I would love to know what your older boy program looks like in your troop,

168
00:12:51,939 --> 00:12:53,920
if you have something similar to this,

169
00:12:54,000 --> 00:12:55,540
or if you're doing it a different way.

170
00:12:55,839 --> 00:12:57,360
I'd love to have you get in touch with me,

171
00:12:57,380 --> 00:12:59,760
and you'll find out how to do that right at the end of the podcast.

172
00:12:59,760 --> 00:13:07,819
Have you ever wandered lonely through the woods?

173
00:13:09,180 --> 00:13:12,660
And everything that feels just as it should

174
00:13:12,660 --> 00:13:18,740
You're part of a life there, you're part of something good

175
00:13:18,740 --> 00:13:23,760
If you've ever wandered lonely through the woods

176
00:13:23,760 --> 00:13:35,400
Find me a letter

177
00:13:35,400 --> 00:13:37,620
Send it by mail

178
00:13:37,620 --> 00:13:40,760
Email, that is, folks.

179
00:13:41,460 --> 00:13:44,360
And here's an answer to one of your emails.

180
00:13:44,360 --> 00:13:56,500
Well, this email came in from Chris in Cranberry Township, Pennsylvania.

181
00:13:56,880 --> 00:14:01,180
He says, hello, I've approached several district executives as well as our council executive,

182
00:14:01,300 --> 00:14:03,920
and I really haven't been able to get a clear-cut answer on my question.

183
00:14:04,760 --> 00:14:11,680
My question is, can an 18-year-old assistant scoutmaster drive youth members to and from camping trips?

184
00:14:11,680 --> 00:14:17,160
Our troop has always shrugged it off and firmly said, no driving scouts until you're 21.

185
00:14:17,780 --> 00:14:22,880
Recently, the troop has started saying an 18-year-old can drive if he has a 21-year-old in the vehicle,

186
00:14:23,000 --> 00:14:26,260
which defeats the purpose because that requires two adults to drive one vehicle.

187
00:14:27,219 --> 00:14:31,760
Any help you could give to clear this up would be greatly appreciated.

188
00:14:32,120 --> 00:14:33,199
Hey, Chris, this one's easy.

189
00:14:33,680 --> 00:14:35,020
Look at the tour permit form.

190
00:14:35,020 --> 00:14:44,740
And a tour permit is a piece of paperwork that gets filled out whenever you're going camping away from your scout reservation or your scout camp.

191
00:14:45,199 --> 00:14:49,640
And it simply describes where you're going, what you're planning on doing,

192
00:14:49,800 --> 00:14:54,840
and making sure that people have the proper training and qualifications to lead the outing.

193
00:14:55,480 --> 00:14:58,960
If you look on the back of the tour permit, there you'll find this statement.

194
00:14:59,620 --> 00:15:04,220
And let's make sure that we're talking about the most recent revision of the tour permit.

195
00:15:04,220 --> 00:15:08,160
If you go online and you just search for scouting tour permit,

196
00:15:08,300 --> 00:15:12,620
you're liable to come up with a dozen different iterations of the tour permit.

197
00:15:13,160 --> 00:15:19,940
So check with your council and make sure that you have the most current edition of the tour permit.

198
00:15:20,180 --> 00:15:27,320
I will have a link to a PDF file of what I am pretty sure is the most current edition of the tour permit.

199
00:15:27,560 --> 00:15:29,960
But like I said, make sure to check with your council.

200
00:15:29,960 --> 00:15:34,040
If you look on the back, it'll say this, driver qualifications.

201
00:15:34,600 --> 00:15:41,460
In part, it says all drivers must have a valid driver's license and must be at least 18 years of age.

202
00:15:41,460 --> 00:15:44,180
There is a youth member exception.

203
00:15:44,180 --> 00:15:54,079
When traveling to an area, regional, or national Boy Scout activity or any venturing event under the leadership of an adult who is 21 years or older,

204
00:15:54,479 --> 00:16:00,959
a youth member at least 16 years of age may be a driver subject to the following conditions.

205
00:16:00,960 --> 00:16:06,280
One, he must have six months driving experience as a licensed driver.

206
00:16:06,900 --> 00:16:10,040
Time on a learner's permit or equivalent is not to be counted.

207
00:16:10,680 --> 00:16:14,920
And two, he has no record of accidents or moving violations.

208
00:16:14,920 --> 00:16:21,660
And three, parental permission has been granted to leader, driver, and riders.

209
00:16:23,060 --> 00:16:33,979
So the answer to your question, Chris, is that the BSA permits a driver who is at least 18 years of age is a qualified driver for a scout outing.

210
00:16:33,980 --> 00:16:45,520
There is a youth member exception if a youth member is at least 16 years old and he meets those criteria, six months driving experience, no record of accidents or moving violations,

211
00:16:45,860 --> 00:16:55,480
and he has his parents' permission to drive, and he also has the permission of any scouts he'll be transporting, he also has the permission of their parents.

212
00:16:55,820 --> 00:16:57,840
He becomes a qualified driver.

213
00:16:57,840 --> 00:17:04,720
One thing that might be confusing the issue in your troop is the too-deep leadership conundrum when it comes to driving.

214
00:17:05,540 --> 00:17:13,960
While two adults, 121 years or older, and a registered BSA leader with youth protection training are required for tour permits,

215
00:17:14,520 --> 00:17:16,620
they are not required in each and every vehicle.

216
00:17:17,319 --> 00:17:23,460
The youth protection concept that goes into effect here is there is no one-on-one contact.

217
00:17:23,700 --> 00:17:27,319
In other words, you don't need to have two adults in every vehicle.

218
00:17:27,839 --> 00:17:37,740
We just don't want one adult and one scout in a vehicle by themselves, unless that is a father and son or mother and son.

219
00:17:38,780 --> 00:17:42,399
More than one scout per vehicle is the way that you solve your problem.

220
00:17:43,159 --> 00:17:46,080
Naturally, a parent can transport their scout to and fro.

221
00:17:46,959 --> 00:17:54,399
So I hope that helps clear that up for you, Chris, and for everyone who's listening, and I appreciate you being in touch.

222
00:17:54,400 --> 00:17:59,340
You can get in touch with your questions and comments, and you're going to find out how in just a moment.

223
00:17:59,340 --> 00:18:08,919
Thanks for listening to this edition of the Scoutmaster Podcast.

224
00:18:09,699 --> 00:18:10,980
And here's how to keep in touch.

225
00:18:11,360 --> 00:18:15,879
You can read the Scoutmaster blog at scoutmaster.typepad.com.

226
00:18:15,880 --> 00:18:20,240
And when you do, stop through and subscribe to the Scoutmaster newsletter.

